  "raw": "{\"avg_delay_min\":15.5}",
  "notes": "Loaded all six carrier files, removed repeated flight_id records (12 duplicate rows), restricted to Jan\u2013Feb 2025, and excluded cancelled flights. Computed actual minus scheduled minutes; treated the 19 large negative clock differences as next-day departures (adding 1,440 minutes), while retaining small negative differences as early departures. The unrounded mean across 1,891 completed flights was 15.4543 minutes."
 },
 {
  "analyst": "S2",
  "raw": "{\"avg_delay_min\": 15.4}",
  "notes": "Loaded all six carrier files, removed repeated flight_id records, excluded 50 cancelled flights, and calculated actual minus scheduled departure minutes for 1,894 completed flights. For documented post-midnight departures, clock differences over 60 minutes negative were adjusted by +1,440 minutes; ordinary early departures remained negative. The resulting mean was 15.4451 minutes, rounded to 1 decimal."
 },
 {
  "analyst": "S3",
  "raw": "{\"avg_delay_min\":15.4}",
  "notes": "Loaded all carrier files, removed repeated flight_id records, computed actual minus scheduled departure minutes (including overnight rollover), excluded cancelled flights, and averaged 1,894 completed flights. The unrounded mean was 15.4451 minutes."
 }
]

{
 "issues": [
  {
   "analyst": "S1",
   "problem": "The reported count (1,891) and mean (15.4543) disagree with S2/S3's 1,894 completed flights and 15.4451 mean. Its initial computation adds 1,440 to every negative raw difference, which would incorrectly convert ordinary early departures to positive delays; although its later code uses a threshold, the threshold-based rollover rule is heuristic and the notes/code are inconsistent."
  },
  {
   "analyst": "S2",
   "problem": "It applies an arbitrary -60-minute cutoff for identifying overnight departures. The documentation says to account for departures after midnight, but does not justify treating every clock difference below -60 as overnight; a moderately early departure could be misclassified. It also does not explicitly restrict records to January-February 2025, though the supplied files are documented as covering that period."
  },
  {
   "analyst": "S3",
   "problem": "It does not explicitly filter to January-February 2025 and excludes only cancelled rows rather than explicitly requiring a nonempty actual departure. Missing actual times become NaN and are silently omitted by the mean, rather than being validated as completed flights. Its -720-minute rollover cutoff is also heuristic and undocumented."
  }
 ],
 "disagreements": "S1 reports 1,891 completed flights and 15.5 minutes, while S2 and S3 agree on 1,894 completed flights and an unrounded mean of 15.4451 minutes, which rounds to 15.4. S1's initial negative-delay adjustment is also inconsistent with its stated treatment of early departures. S2 uses a -60-minute rollover threshold; S1 and S3 use -720 minutes. Based on the agreement and the matching completed-flight count, S2/S3 are better supported, although the rollover and completion checks should be made explicit and validated against the records.",
 "best": "S3",
 "correction": "Use the completed, non-cancelled flights in the January-February 2025 files, require nonempty actual_dep, preserve genuinely early departures as negative, and correct only documented post-midnight departures. The supported final value is {\"avg_delay_min\":15.4}."
}
